---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import NnMasthead from '../../components/NnMasthead.astro';
import race from '../../content/race.json';
import { orTbc } from '@src/shared/privacy';

// The same event theme as the forms it belongs to — see packages/shared/styles/nn-theme.css.
//
// **The horror stops at the edge of the card.** The page furniture is the campaign's, so
// somebody arriving from a form knows they have not left it; the notice itself is ink on
// white, Inter throughout, with nothing decorative anywhere near the legal text.
import '@src/shared/styles/nn-theme.css';

// The race's privacy notice, replacing the one written when `/nn/` collected a name, an
// email address and a consent box and nothing else. It covers the entry form as well, so
// it has to describe fourteen fields, a payment, a special category of data under its own
// consent, and five retention periods.
//
// **It is no longer the club's only notice.** `/privacy/` is the site-wide one — the club,
// the account, and everything that is not about a race — and this page is the race half:
// the medical note, the emergency contact, the payment, and the retention period
// `packages/db/tests/entries-retention.test.ts` ties to `entries.events.medical_retention`.
// The two link to each other, and the split is argued at the head of `src/pages/privacy.astro`.
// The four values below stay this file's, and `nn-privacy.spec.ts` still counts exactly four
// of them.
//
// **This page is written from the schema, not from the form.** The list in section 2 is what
// `entries.entry_purchases`, `entries.entrants` and `entries.entrant_medical` actually hold —
// which is four rows more than a list of what somebody types, because the payment reference,
// the consent version and the timestamps are recorded about a person and none of them is
// typed. Under-listing what a controller processes is a defect in a notice, and it is the
// defect this page exists to not have.
//
// ## The four things that are genuinely undecided
//
// Who somebody writes to, how long an entry is kept, whether an email address is kept to
// tell people about next year, and what is true about photographs. They live in
// `src/content/race.json` as `null` alongside the race facts, for the same reason — one file
// to edit when an answer arrives, and an honest "to be confirmed by the club" in the meantime
// rather than a plausible-looking invention. `orTbc` is the whole mechanism, and
// `nn-privacy.spec.ts` counts the marker — four, exactly — so filling one in fails that test
// until it is updated in the same commit, and a settled fact quietly reverting to the marker
// fails it too.
//
// **A wrong answer on this page is a legal claim, not a typo.** See
// docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/nn/privacy/';

// **`orTbc` moved to `@src/shared/privacy` when the second notice arrived**, and only because
// there is a second notice: the marker is a sentence a reader is meant to recognise on both
// pages as "nobody has answered this yet", and two copies of it is two chances for one to be
// reworded on its own. Nothing about what it does here changed — every interpolated value
// goes through it, including the five that are settled, so emptying one by accident renders a
// sentence somebody notices rather than a sentence with a hole in it. The sentences around
// them are written to read correctly either way, which is why section 4 no longer lower-cases
// what it is given.

const { privacy } = race;
---

<Base
  title="What the club does with your details — Nightingale Nightmare"
  description="What Southville Running Club collects when you enter Nightingale Nightmare or register your interest, why, who else sees it, and how long it is kept."
  canonical={canonical}
  theme="nn"
>
  {
    /* The campaign navigation, as on the other four pages. Somebody who opens the privacy
    notice to check what is held before typing their name should not have to retrace their
    steps to carry on. */
  }
  <NnMasthead slot="masthead" />

  <header class="nn-hero nn-hero-compact">
    <div class="nn-wrap">
      <h1>What the club does with your details</h1>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="nn-wrap">
    <div class="nn-card nn-prose">
      {
        /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** A newline
        either side of an expression is collapsed away rather than kept as a space, so a
        paragraph reads "…Nightingale Nightmareand nothing else" without it. Keeping the
        expression on one line with the words around it would fix it too — until the next
        reflow past 90 characters silently breaks it again. */
      }
      {
        /* The link up, and the lede is where it belongs: somebody who opened this page to
        find out what the club does with their details has to be told in the first sentence
        if the answer for the thing they are asking about is on a different page. */
      }
      <p class="lede">
        This covers the entry form and the register-your-interest form for{' '}
        {race.name}. Everything else the club does with your details — including an
        account on this website — is in <a href="/privacy/">the club's privacy notice</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Who we are</h2>

      <p>
        {orTbc(privacy.controller)} is the data controller for the information you give us when
        you enter the {race.name}, or when you ask to hear when entries open.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Registered office:</strong> {orTbc(privacy.registeredOffice)}</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Registered in England and Wales</strong>, company number{' '}
          {orTbc(privacy.companyNumber)}
        </li>
        {
          /* **`{' '}` again, and Prettier is what puts it back.** Formatting this file moves a
          bare `{expression}` onto a line of its own, and a newline either side of one is
          collapsed rather than kept as a space — so this rendered
          "Contact about your data:To be confirmed by the club" the first time it was built.
          The explicit space survives the reformat; a space in the source does not.
          `nn-privacy.spec.ts` asserts both of these joins as whole strings. */
        }
        <li>
          <strong>Contact about your data:</strong>{' '}
          {orTbc(privacy.contact)}
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        If you are unhappy with how we have handled your information, please tell us first
        — we would rather put it right. You also have the right to complain to the
        Information Commissioner's Office at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/">ico.org.uk</a>,
        or on 0303 123 1113.
      </p>

      <h2>2. What we collect, and why</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>If you ask to hear when entries open</strong>, we collect your name and
        email address, we record that you agreed to us contacting you, and we record the
        date and time you asked. We use it for one thing: a single email when entries
        open. It is not a mailing list and we will not use it for anything else without
        asking you again.
      </p>

      <p><strong>If you enter the race</strong>, we collect:</p>

      {
        /* Real tables throughout, and two columns at most — see the tables block in
        nn-theme.css for why the third column was folded in and what makes this safe at
        320px. The roles are restated because the stacked layout below 30rem drops the
        implicit ones. */
      }
      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">What</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Why we need it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Your name</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To enter you, to put you on the start list, and to publish your result
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Your email address</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To send your confirmation and any information about race day
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Your date of birth</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To work out your age category, and to check you are 18 or over on race day
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Your race category</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              Used with your date of birth to work out your category, and to group the
              results and the prizes
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              How you describe your gender, if you tell us
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              Kept with your entry so the club knows who its runners are. It is never
              published, never used to work out your category, and you do not have to
              answer
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Your running club, if you have one</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              Shown alongside your result, as is normal at a race
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Your England Athletics number, if you claim the affiliated entry
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To check the discounted entry is one you are entitled to
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              An emergency contact's name and phone number
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              So we can reach someone on your behalf if something happens on race day
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Anything you tell us in the medical box</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              So our first aiders know, if they need to. This is optional — see section 4
            </td>
          </tr>
          {
            /* **Two rows for the guide, and they are here because the schema holds them.**
            A visually impaired runner enters their guide on the same entry, so
            `entries.entrants` holds a second person's name, date of birth, category and
            emergency contact, and `entries.entrant_medical` may hold their note. A notice
            that listed only the runner would under-list what the club processes about a real
            person who never filled anything in themselves.

            **The declaration is data about disability**, which is special category under
            Article 9 exactly as the medical box is — so it is named separately rather than
            folded into "your entry", and section 4's consent is what makes it lawful. See
            ADR-022. */
          }
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              That you are a visually impaired runner, if you tell us
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              So we know a guide is running with you and can plan for both of you. This is
              optional, and it is special category data — see section 4
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Your guide's name, date of birth, email address and emergency contact
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              Your guide is on the course, so we need much the same details about them as
              about you — including their own address, so we can send them the race
              information, and somebody to contact if something happens to them.
              <strong>We do not ask their race category</strong>, because guides are not
              placed and are in no age category
            </td>
          </tr>
          {
            /* **The four rows below were derived from the schema rather than approved.** The
            approved draft listed what somebody types; these are the rest of what the tables
            hold, and a notice that omits them under-lists what the club processes. They go to
            the committee with the four open decisions. */
          }
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader"
              >The entry type you chose, and what you paid</th
            >
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To take the right fee and to have a record of the transaction
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">A payment reference from Stripe</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              So a payment can be matched to your entry, and refunded if it has to be
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Which boxes you ticked, and the wording you ticked them under
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              So the club can show what you actually agreed to, even if the wording
              changes later
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              When you entered, and when your payment was confirmed
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Why we need it">
              To administer entries and to answer questions about them afterwards
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        We do not ask for anything else. Alongside what you type, we keep the record of
        your payment and of when you entered — set out above — and nothing beyond it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>We never see your card details.</strong> Payment is handled by Stripe, who take
        your card information directly. We are told that a payment succeeded, for how much,
        and a reference to match it to your entry.
      </p>

      <h2>3. What we are allowed to do with it, and why that is lawful</h2>

      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">What we do</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">Our lawful basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Take your entry and give you a place</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Performing our contract with you
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Send you your confirmation and race-day information
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Performing our contract with you
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Work out your age category and place you in results
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Performing our contract with you
            </td>
          </tr>
          {
            /* Derived from the schema alongside the four rows in section 2, and for the same
            reason: an item the club collects with no stated basis is the same under-listing
            in a different table. */
          }
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Keep a record of your payment, including Stripe's reference
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Performing our contract with you, and our legitimate interest in keeping
              accounts that can be reconciled
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Publish results — your name, club, category and time
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Our legitimate interest in running a race and publishing its results, which
              is what entrants expect a race to do
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Check an England Athletics number against the club's records
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Our legitimate interest in charging the right entry fee
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Contact your emergency contact if something happens to you
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis"
              >Protecting your vital interests</td
            >
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Keep records of who took part</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Our legitimate interest in being able to answer questions about the event
              afterwards, including for insurance and our race permit
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Email you when entries open, if you asked us to
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Your consent, which you can withdraw at any time
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Hold what you tell us in the medical box</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="Our lawful basis">
              Your explicit consent — see section 4
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        We do not use your information for marketing, we do not profile you, and nothing
        about your entry is decided automatically in a way that affects you.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Medical information — handled separately, on purpose</h2>

      <p>
        Anything you write in the medical box is <strong>special category data</strong> under
        UK data protection law, which means it gets more protection than the rest of your entry.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is optional.</strong> You can enter without it.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is only held if you tick the box</strong> giving explicit consent. If you
          leave that box unticked, nothing you typed is stored at all — it never reaches our
          records.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who sees it:</strong> the club's first aiders and the race organisers, on
          race day. Nobody else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How long we keep it:</strong>{' '}
          {orTbc(privacy.medicalRetention)}. It is then deleted — separately from, and
          sooner than, the rest of your entry.
        </li>
        <li>
          It is held apart from the rest of your entry in our system, so deleting it is a
          single action rather than something anyone has to remember to do field by field.
        </li>
      </ul>

      {
        /* **The same protection, said in the same section, for the same legal reason.** A
        declaration that somebody is visually impaired is data about disability, which is
        special category under Article 9 exactly as a medical note is — so it is consented to
        explicitly, seen only by the volunteers who need it, and never published. See
        ADR-022. */
      }
      <p>
        <strong
          >Telling us you are a visually impaired runner is special category data too</strong
        >, for the same reason, and it is handled the same way.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is optional.</strong> You can enter without telling us.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who sees it:</strong> the race organisers, so they can plan for you and your
          guide. It is not on the results, it is not on any list we publish, and it is not in
          anything we export.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your guide's details are part of your entry</strong>, and are kept for
          as long as it is. Anything your guide tells us in the medical box is held and
          deleted on exactly the same terms as yours.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Who else sees your information</h2>

      <p>
        We do not sell your information and we do not share it for marketing. It is seen
        by:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The club volunteers who run the race</strong> — the entries, results and first
          aid teams.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Stripe</strong>, who take your payment. They hold your card details; we
          do not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Supabase</strong>, who host our database. It is stored in London (AWS{
            ' '
          }
          <code>eu-west-2</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resend</strong>, who send our emails — your confirmation when you enter,
          and a message if your entry is cancelled or transferred.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The public</strong>, but only your name, club, category and finishing
          time, in the published results.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Our race is run under an ARC permit, so we may have to share limited details with
        the permitting body if there is an incident.
      </p>

      <h2>6. How long we keep it</h2>

      <table role="table">
        <thead role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">What</th>
            <th scope="col" role="columnheader">How long, and why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody role="rowgroup">
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Medical notes</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long">
              {orTbc(privacy.medicalRetention)}
              <span class="nn-why">
                Only needed for race day and immediately afterwards
              </span>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Your entry — name, contact, date of birth, emergency contact
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long">{orTbc(privacy.entryRetention)}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Published results — name, club, category, time
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long">
              Kept indefinitely
              <span class="nn-why">
                Results are the public record of a sporting event
              </span>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">
              Your email address, to tell you about next year's race
            </th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long">{orTbc(privacy.emailRetention)}</td>
          </tr>
          <tr role="row">
            <th scope="row" role="rowheader">Interest-list sign-ups</th>
            <td role="cell" data-label="How long">
              Deleted once entries have opened and the email has been sent, unless you
              have entered
              <span class="nn-why">It exists to send one email</span>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        When a retention period ends we delete the information, or strip out anything that
        identifies you so that what is left is no longer about you.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Your rights</h2>

      <p>You can ask us to:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>give you a copy of what we hold about you;</li>
        <li>correct anything that is wrong;</li>
        <li>delete what we hold, where we no longer need it;</li>
        <li>stop or limit what we do with it;</li>
        <li>send it to you, or to someone else, in a portable form.</li>
      </ul>

      {
        /* **"Removed" has to appear on this page in those words.** Both forms link here
        promising "how to have it removed" — `NnEntryForm.astro` and `nn/index.astro` — and a
        page that only ever says "delete" makes the link that brought somebody here slightly
        untrue. `nn-privacy.spec.ts` holds the link text and this sentence to each other. */
      }
      <p>
        <strong>Asking us to delete what we hold is how your details are removed.</strong> You
        do not have to give a reason, and it does not affect entering the race.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where we rely on your consent — the interest list, the medical box, and telling us
        you are a visually impaired runner — you can withdraw it at any time, and we will
        act on that.
      </p>

      <p>
        Write to us using the contact in section 1. We may ask you to confirm who you are
        before we hand over personal information, which is a protection for you rather
        than an obstacle.
      </p>

      <h2>8. Photographs</h2>

      {
        /* **The draft's paragraph is not printed here, and that is deliberate.** What the
        club does with race photographs was marked for the committee to confirm rather than
        written down, so printing a description of it would be this page inventing the
        answer — which is the one thing it must not do. */
      }
      <p><strong>{orTbc(privacy.photographs)}</strong></p>

      <p>
        Photographs are taken at the race. What the club does with them — whether they are
        published, where, and for how long — is with the committee, and this page is
        updated when it is settled.
      </p>

      <h2>9. Changes to this notice</h2>

      <p>
        If we change how we use your information we will update this page, and we will
        tell you directly if the change is one you would want to know about.
      </p>

      <p>Last updated: {orTbc(privacy.lastUpdated)}</p>
    </div>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Southville Running Club. Anything above reading "to be confirmed by the club" is
        with the committee, and this page is updated when it is settled.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/nn/">Back to {race.name}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/privacy/">The club's privacy notice</a>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  {
    /* The same decorative artwork as the forms' page, and empty for the same reason — it is
    a CSS background. See the artwork section of nn-theme.css. */
  }
  <div class="nn-hands" aria-hidden="true"></div>
</Base>
